  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>Let us say that the first wave has a zero phase, then the second one is ahead of the first by a phase of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">π</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math>&nbsp;and so the third one is ahead of the first one by a phase of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">π</mi></math>. So the first and the third are opposite to each other. Hence,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msqrt><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mn>11</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></msqrt></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi>mm</mi></math></p>",
    "image": null
  },
